===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|hog}}, from {{inh|en|ang|hogg}}, {{m|ang|hocg||hog}}, possibly from {{etyl|non|en}} {{m|non|hǫggva||to strike, chop, cut|sc=Latinx}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*hawwaną||to hew, forge}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*kowə-||to beat, hew, forge}}. Cognate with Old High German {{m|goh|houwan}}, Old Saxon {{m|osx|hauwan}}, Old English {{m|ang|hēawan}} (English {{m|en|hew}}). "Hog" originally meant a [[castrate]]d male pig, hence a sense of "the cut one". (Compare "[[hogget]]" for a castrated male sheep.) More at {{l|en|hew}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# Any animal belonging to the [[Suidae]] family of mammals, especially the [[pig]], the [[warthog]], and the [[boar]].
# {{lb|en|specifically}} An adult swine {{qualifier|contrasted with a [[pig]], a young swine}}.
#* '''2005''' April, ''Live Swine from Canada, Investigation No. 731-TA-1076 (Final), publication 3766, April 2005, U.S. International Trade Commission'' {{ISBN|1457819899}}, page I-9:
#*: Weanlings grow into feeder pigs, and feeder pigs grow into slaughter '''hogs'''. {{...}} Ultimately the end use for virtually all pigs and '''hogs''' is to be slaughtered for the production of pork and other products.
# A [[greedy]] person; one who refuses to share.
# {{lb|en|slang}} A large motorcycle, particularly a [[w:Harley Davidson|Harley-Davidson]].
# {{lb|en|UK}} A young [[sheep]] that has not been [[shear|shorn]].
# {{lb|en|nautical}} A rough, flat scrubbing broom for scrubbing a ship's bottom under water.
#: {{rfquotek|Totten}}
# A device for mixing and stirring the [[pulp]] from which [[paper]] is made.
# {{lb|en|UK|historical|archaic|_|slang|countable|and|uncountable}} A [[shilling]] [[coin]]; its [[value]], 12 [[old pence]].
#* '''1933''', {{w|George Orwell}}, ''{{w|Down and Out in Paris and London}}'', xxix
#*: ‘[['ere|’Ere]] [[y'|y’]]are, the best [[rig out|rig-out]] you ever [['ad|’ad]]. A [[tosheroon]] [half a crown]<!--sic: in original--> for the coat, two '''[['og|’og]]s''' for the [[trousers]], one and a [[tanner]] for the boots, and a '''[['og|’og]]''' for the cap and scarf. That’s seven [[bob]].’
#* '''1961''', Eric Partridge, ''The Routledge Dictionary of Historical Slang''
#*: '''hog''' ([[plural|pl]] '''hog'''). A [[shilling]]: [[originally|orig.]] ([[circa|ca]] 1670), [[cant|c]].; in [[century|C]].19–20, low [[slang|s]].
# {{lb|en|UK|historical|obsolete|_|slang|countable|_|&|_|uncountable}} A [[tanner]], a [[sixpence]] [[coin]]; its [[value]].
#* '''1961''', Eric Partridge, ''The Routledge Dictionary of Historical Slang''
#*: '''hog''' ([[plural|pl]] '''hog''')... 2. In [[century|C]].18–early 19, [[occasionally|occ]]. a [[sixpence]]: also [[cant|c]]., whence the U.S. sense. [[probably|Prob]]. [[from|ex]] the figure of a hog on a small silver coin.
# {{lb|en|UK|historical|obsolete|_|slang|countable|_|&|_|uncountable}} A [[half-crown]] [[coin]]; its [[value]], 30 old [[pence]].
#* '''1961''', Eric Partridge, ''The Routledge Dictionary of Historical Slang''
#*: '''hog''' ([[plural|pl]] '''hog''')... 3. A [[half-crown]]: [[circa|ca]] 1860–1910.

